\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The onsite action in scalar frames}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For an \vocabk{orbit section}{fdip-010X}, transforming by the
inverse site frames sends the \vocabk{onsite Yukawa operator}{fdip-011M}
to the constant fibre operator #{Y(\phi^\circ)} in every cell
slot and the connection to its reduced links. The reference
operator commutes with #{\rho_\psi(G^\circ)}. On any supplied
fibre summand where #{Y(\phi^\circ)} acts by a scalar #{y_j},
the onsite term including the bare mass acts by #{m+y_j}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{g_x=\orbitlift(\phi_x)}. The inverse frame gauge
sends #{\phi_x} to #{\phi^\circ} and each link to
#{g_{h(e)}^{-1}U_e g_{s(e)}} by \ref{fdip-010Y}. Apply
\ref{fdip-011N}. For any #{a\in G^\circ}, the identity
#{a\phi^\circ=\phi^\circ} gives
#{\rho_\psi(a)Y(\phi^\circ)\rho_\psi(a)^{-1}=Y(\phi^\circ)}.
Adding #{m\bbI} on a summand of scalar action gives its asserted
onsite value.}

\p{Commutation alone leaves multiplicity spaces available to the
endomorphism. For example the reference stabilizer in
\ref{fdip-011O} is trivial, yet the exchange can have two different
eigenvalues on the same trivial isotypic component. To split the
full operator into such summands one also needs the cochain term
to preserve them; a negative link in that example can interchange
the eigenlines.}
}
